# Creative Asset Manager autonomous workflow

Follow `AGENTS.md` first. This rule adds a stricter delivery lane for Cursor Agent / My Machines.

## Allowed autonomous lane

- Work only in a non-production checkout intended for Cursor/My Machines.
- Create or reuse a task branch. Do not make feature changes directly on `main`.
- Inspect, edit, build, test, and run development/staging services without asking for routine approval.
- For UI work, use Browser to verify the changed flow at a loopback or explicitly designated staging URL. Capture the page state, console errors, and failed requests relevant to the change.
- Run `bash scripts/cam-cursor-staging-gate.sh` before declaring work ready. Use `CAM_STAGING_FULL=1` for architecture, migration, authorization, storage, pipeline, provider, or other cross-cutting changes.
- Keep changes scoped. Preserve the repository security and architecture contracts.

## Production boundary

Cursor Agent must NOT:

- run `scripts/deploy-cam-frontend.sh`, `scripts/cam-rebuild-backend.sh`, or any other production deploy/rollback command;
- use `sudo`, mutate systemd/Nginx production state, or write under `/etc/creative-asset-manager`, `/opt/creative-asset-manager`, or `/var/www/creative-asset-manager`;
- read or source production secrets;
- connect to or mutate the production PostgreSQL/Elasticsearch state for development or testing;
- perform destructive migrations, production data repair, IAM/DNS/firewall changes, or bulk source-asset deletion;
- publish a Windows desktop release from Linux.

Production web/API deployment and Windows desktop packaging remain CodeLocal/operator actions after an explicit current user instruction.

## Browser boundary

- Prefer local development or isolated staging endpoints.
- Never use Browser to perform a mutating production action.
- If a browser check needs authenticated state, use staging/test credentials only.
- Treat console/network failures as verification evidence; do not bypass security controls to make a browser check pass.

## Handoff contract

When work is ready, report:

1. branch and commit (or dirty working-tree status if not committed);
2. files changed and behavior changed;
3. tests/checks actually run and their result;
4. Browser verification performed and any console/network errors;
5. migration/dependency/secret impact;
6. known risks and rollback notes;
7. whether production deployment or native Windows build is requested next.

Do not claim production is deployed unless CodeLocal/operator deployment actually completed and was verified.
